{
  "conversation_lines": [
    {
      "turn_number": 1,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. I’m Dr. Lee, one of the surgical team. You were admitted early this morning for epigastric pain. Can you tell me more about what’s been going on?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 2,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, thank you. I’ve had this burning pain in my upper belly for about a day now. It’s worse after I eat. I also felt nauseous and threw up once, but it was just food—nothing strange."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 3,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "I see. Any fever, chills, or changes in your bowel movements?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 4,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No fever. But my stools have been looser than usual—three times today, all soft and not bloody. I’m still passing gas, at least."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 5,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Okay, that’s good to hear. You had your gastric bypass surgery a few months ago and lost about 50 pounds, correct?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 6,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Yes, about three months ago. I’ve been doing well until now. No pain or issues before this."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 7,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That helps. We’re going to run some tests to find out what’s causing this pain. We’ve already ordered blood work and a CT scan with contrast to look at your stomach and the bypass area."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 8,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is this something serious? Could it be related to the surgery?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 9,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It could be. One possibility is an ulcer near the connection site. We’ll know more after the CT and a scope procedure later today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 10,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A scope? Like an endoscopy?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 11,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Yes, exactly. We’ll pass a small camera through your mouth to look at the stomach pouch and the connection to the small intestine. It’s called an EGD."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 12,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, that makes sense. Will it hurt?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 13,
      "time": "2169-04-03 04:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’ll be sedated, so you won’t feel much. Just some pressure. We’ll do it after the CT scan."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 14,
      "time": "2169-04-03 10:15:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi again. Your CT scan is back. It shows contrast in both your gastric pouch and the remnant stomach, which suggests there might be a small connection between them—possibly a fistula."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 15,
      "time": "2169-04-03 10:16:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "A fistula? What does that mean?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 16,
      "time": "2169-04-03 10:17:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It means there’s an abnormal passage between two areas that shouldn’t be connected. In your case, between parts of your stomach. It’s not common, but it can happen after surgery."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 17,
      "time": "2169-04-03 10:18:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Could that be causing the pain and ulcers?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 18,
      "time": "2169-04-03 10:19:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It could contribute. We’re about to do the EGD to look more closely. We’ll also check for ulcers and take a blood test for H. pylori, a bacteria that can cause ulcers."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 19,
      "time": "2169-04-03 10:20:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I’m ready when you are."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 20,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Hi, you’re awake. The EGD is done. We found two ulcers near the connection site—on the jejunal side, about 2 cm past the anastomosis."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 21,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Oh no. Are they bad?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 22,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "One is 8 mm and superficial, the other is 1.5 cm and deeper, with a dark spot in the center. But there’s no active bleeding, which is good."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 23,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Is that the fistula?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 24,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not exactly. The fistula is between the pouch and remnant stomach. The ulcers are downstream, near where the stomach connects to the intestine. They may be related, though."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 25,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "What causes ulcers like this after bypass?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 26,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:06:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Several things—like acid exposure, certain medications, or even smoking. But you don’t smoke, right?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 27,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:07:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "No, never smoked."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 28,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:08:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good. We also sent a blood test for H. pylori. It came back negative, so that’s not the cause."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 29,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:09:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "So what now?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 30,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:10:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll start you on a strong acid-reducing medication called Protonix, given through your IV. It helps ulcers heal. We’ll also control your pain with IV morphine for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 31,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:11:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need another surgery?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 32,
      "time": "2169-04-03 13:12:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not right now. Most of these ulcers heal with medication and diet changes. We’ll monitor you closely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 33,
      "time": "2169-04-04 09:00:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good morning. How’s the pain today?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 34,
      "time": "2169-04-04 09:01:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Better, actually. Still some discomfort, but not as sharp. The morphine helps."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 35,
      "time": "2169-04-04 09:02:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Great. We’re going to switch your pain medicine to oral Roxicet and start you on a bariatric stage 2 diet—soft foods like mashed potatoes and scrambled eggs."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 36,
      "time": "2169-04-04 09:03:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Can I eat solid food again?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 37,
      "time": "2169-04-04 09:04:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Not yet. We need to let the ulcers heal. You’ll stay on soft foods for now, then advance to stage 3 later."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 38,
      "time": "2169-04-04 09:05:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Okay, I can do that."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 39,
      "time": "2169-04-05 08:45:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Morning. Your pain is still improving, and you’ve tolerated the soft diet well. We’re going to advance you to bariatric stage 3—more solid foods, but still easy to digest."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 40,
      "time": "2169-04-05 08:46:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s good. But I’ve been having loose stools in the evenings. Is that normal?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 41,
      "time": "2169-04-05 08:47:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "It could be from the changes in your digestion after surgery. We’ll switch you to a lactose-free diet to see if that helps. Sometimes dairy can cause diarrhea."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 42,
      "time": "2169-04-05 08:48:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I didn’t think about that. I had some yogurt yesterday."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 43,
      "time": "2169-04-05 08:49:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "That could be it. We’ll hold off on dairy for now. Also, we’re switching your Protonix from IV to oral. You’ll take it by mouth twice a day."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 44,
      "time": "2169-04-05 08:50:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Will I need to take that forever?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 45,
      "time": "2169-04-05 08:51:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "At least for a few months. We’ll reassess at your follow-up. The goal is to let the ulcers heal completely."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 46,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:30:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Good news. You’ve done really well. Pain is minimal, you’re eating well, and your labs are stable. We’re going to discharge you today."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 47,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:31:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "That’s great. What do I need to do at home?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 48,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:32:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Continue the omeprazole twice a day—crush the pill and mix it with applesauce. Take the oxycodone only if you need it for pain. And stay on the lactose-free diet for now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 49,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:33:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "When can I go back to work?"
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 50,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:34:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Once your pain is controlled without narcotics. I’ll give you a note. Follow up with your surgeon in two weeks."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 51,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:35:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you so much. I’m glad it wasn’t something worse."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 52,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:36:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "You’re welcome. You handled this really well. Call us if the pain worsens or if you notice black stools or vomiting."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 53,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:37:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will. And I’ll keep taking my vitamins and CPAP at night."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 54,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:38:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Perfect. Those are important. Take care, and we’ll see you soon."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 55,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:39:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you, Doctor."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 56,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:40:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "One more thing—avoid heavy lifting and straws. They can increase pressure on the stomach."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 57,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:41:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Got it. No straws, no heavy lifting."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 58,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:42:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Exactly. You’re all set."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 59,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:43:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I feel ready to go home now."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 60,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:44:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’re ready to discharge you. The nurse will go over your medications and paperwork."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 61,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:45:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you again for explaining everything."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 62,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:46:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Of course. Take care and rest well."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 63,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:47:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "I will."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 64,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:48:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "We’ll see you in follow-up."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 65,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:49:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Looking forward to it."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 66,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:50:00",
      "speaker": "Doctor",
      "text": "Have a safe trip home."
    },
    {
      "turn_number": 67,
      "time": "2169-04-06 07:51:00",
      "speaker": "Patient",
      "text": "Thank you."
    }
  ],
  "summary": {
    "admission_start": "2169-04-03 04:06:00",
    "admission_end": "2169-04-06 15:55:00",
    "summary_paragraph": "The patient was admitted for evaluation of epigastric pain three months after Roux-en-Y gastric bypass surgery. She reported nausea, one episode of non-bloody vomiting, and looser stools. A CT scan revealed oral contrast in both the gastric pouch and remnant stomach, suggesting a fistulous connection, along with mild dilatation of the proximal efferent limb. An EGD confirmed two perianastomotic ulcers on the jejunal side with no active bleeding, and an H. pylori serology test was negative. She was managed with IV Protonix and pain control using IV morphine, later transitioned to oral omeprazole and Roxicet. Her diet was advanced from clear liquids to bariatric stage III, with a switch to lactose-free to address diarrhea. She tolerated oral intake and medications well, with controlled pain and stable vitals, leading to discharge home with follow-up instructions and a two-week surgical clinic appointment.",
    "problems": [
      "Gastrojejunal ulcer, unspecified as acute or chronic, without mention of hemorrhage or perforation, without mention of obstruction",
      "Obstructive sleep apnea (adult)(pediatric)",
      "Esophageal reflux",
      "Polycystic ovaries",
      "Bariatric surgery status"
    ]
  }
}
